# Build-context excludes for BOTH images. Both build from the repository root, because
# each workspace imports @booking/shared through the root npm workspace link.
#
# This file exists in addition to the per-Dockerfile `backend/Dockerfile.dockerignore` and
# `frontend/Dockerfile.dockerignore`, and it is the one that actually protects you:
#
#   - BuildKit prefers `<dockerfile-path>.dockerignore` when present and ignores this file.
#   - Every other builder — Podman/Buildah, and Docker with BuildKit disabled — looks only
#     for `.dockerignore` at the context root, and silently sends everything when it is
#     absent.
#
# "Silently" is the problem. With no root ignore file, `node_modules` alone is ~225 MB and
# ~15k files; pushing that across the Windows→WSL boundary makes `load build context` look
# like a hang rather than an error, with no message saying why. That is exactly how it
# presented before this file existed.
#
# So keep the rules here a superset-safe baseline. The per-Dockerfile files may exclude
# more (each drops the other workspace), never less.

# Dependencies — reinstalled inside the image, and by far the largest thing here.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output — regenerated in the image; a stale host build must never leak in.
**/dist
storybook-static
**/storybook-static

# Version control and tooling state
**/.git
.git
.github
.claude
.vscode

# Local environment. The example is kept: it is documentation, not a secret.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# Test and log noise
**/coverage
**/*.log
**/.vitest
**/.vite

# Repository documentation that no image needs
planning
scripts/local

# Stray host artefacts
*.exe
